1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,740
In Jesus' name I pray, amen.

2
00:00:01,740 --> 00:00:05,020
I'm gonna sit on a stool today

3
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:07,260
because I do not feel very well.

4
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:10,840
And that's the story.

5
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:14,380
And so it's a great reminder

6
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:18,460
that the Holy Spirit does not need me to be energetic

7
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:20,800
to speak through me to you.

8
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,120
And so that's my prayer this morning

9
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,100
that we would hear from him.

10
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:27,200
Hey, being a father has opened me up

11
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,100
to many new experiences

12
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,020
and a whole new world of information.

13
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:32,080
When you become a parent,

14
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:33,500
you're exposed to things

15
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:35,420
that you never would have been exposed to before.

16
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:37,520
I have been experiencing that

17
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:38,820
for about a year and a half now.

18
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:41,200
One of those new experiences

19
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,320
that have impacted me greatly

20
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:47,420
is an Australian TV show by the name of Bluey.

21
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:50,700
Some of you may be familiar.

22
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:52,860
And if you are not, I am sorry.

23
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:54,880
It is your loss.

24
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:57,360
From the outside looking in,

25
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:58,940
it is a silly show for kids

26
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:01,580
about a young blue healer puppy and her family.

27
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:03,140
Yes, her family.

28
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:04,500
My dad found out it was a girl

29
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:05,500
and he was very upset.

30
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,940
But once you get enveloped into the family,

31
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:11,900
you quickly realize that in my opinion,

32
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,040
it is a cinematic masterpiece

33
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:17,120
filled with lighthearted fun for the kids

34
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:19,040
and deep philosophical reflections

35
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,420
for the life or for the parents.

36
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:26,640
One such episode of Cinematic Masterpiece

37
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:29,040
is an episode called Born Yesterday.

38
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,300
Anybody?

39
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:32,640
Are my parents in the room?

40
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:33,680
All right, Born Yesterday.

41
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:36,700
The dad pretends that he was born yesterday

42
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:39,960
and he is experiencing everything for the first time.

43
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,200
It's obviously goofy for the kids.

44
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:43,440
They're walking him all around

45
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,220
and teaching him about what the sun is

46
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:47,220
and why you shouldn't just walk up to strangers

47
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:48,100
and eat their food.

48
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:50,900
Goofy for the kids,

49
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:53,840
but it has deep rooted meaning for adults.

50
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:54,780
At the end of the episode,

51
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:55,940
after the game is over

52
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:57,360
and the dad has stopped playing,

53
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:59,760
the mom looks out into the backyard

54
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:02,880
and the son is, or the father,

55
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,720
sorry, I gotta do my do not disturb.

56
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:05,860
My group chat's going off.

57
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:06,220
Somebody.

58
00:02:12,860 --> 00:02:16,540
The wife looks out

59
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:18,760
and the dad is sitting out in the grass by himself,

60
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:21,460
marveling at the beauty of creation all around him,

61
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:23,020
the creation that he walks by

62
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:24,600
every single day without noticing.

63
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,640
But just that few minutes of a game

64
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:29,960
of making him look at everything

65
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:31,860
like he's looking at it for the first time

66
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:35,120
opened up his eyes to the beauty all around him.

67
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:38,760
Approaching familiar things from a fresh perspective

68
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:41,760
can often lead us to a deeper appreciation.

69
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:44,760
And that's what I wanna do with communion this morning

70
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:46,900
because my guess is that there's really two groups

71
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:48,280
of people in the room this morning.

72
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:50,380
There are people who are newer to faith

73
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:53,880
who have kind of been outside the church looking in

74
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:55,260
and have found a home here.

75
00:02:55,260 --> 00:02:57,560
And my guess is for you,

76
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,380
communion feels a bit strange,

77
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:02,780
but everybody else seems to be comfortable with it.

78
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:05,180
So you've just learned to go along with the flow

79
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:06,520
and not ask any questions.

80
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:11,040
And then for a lot of others of us,

81
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:12,160
we're longtime believers

82
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:14,320
or what I like to call church folk

83
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,640
because for whatever reason, that's how you say it.

84
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:21,380
And we have become familiar

85
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:22,980
with participating in communion

86
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:26,280
and maybe we even have a shallow understanding of it.

87
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:28,420
We have kind of just adopted

88
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:30,000
whatever we were taught as children.

89
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:31,280
We haven't really thought about it

90
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:32,860
for ourselves in a long time

91
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:36,200
and we've lost our appreciation and wonder

92
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:40,880
around what is so beautiful and great about communion.

93
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:42,200
So that's my goal

94
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,060
is that we'd look at it from a fresh perspective.

95
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:46,160
And for those of you who have been too afraid

96
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:48,220
to ask what's going on with this whole thing

97
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:49,420
because it seems strange to you,

98
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:51,620
but everyone else thinks it's normal,

99
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:53,920
hopefully this will help you understand

100
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,480
what it is exactly we are doing in communion.

101
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:59,440
And for those of us that have grown up in the church

102
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:02,180
and we've become familiar with it

103
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:04,840
or we've just embraced whatever theology

104
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:06,780
about communion was handed to us,

105
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:08,840
I hope that taking this fresh look

106
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:10,900
will invite you into a deeper experience

107
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:13,540
at the table this morning and moving forward.

108
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:14,780
Does that sound like a good plan?

109
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:17,280
All right, so the outline for this morning

110
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:19,500
is what is communion exactly?

111
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:23,880
And then why of all the things,

112
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:26,320
and this was the question that I actually received

113
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:28,280
from the survey of the frequently asked questions

114
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:31,340
was of all the things Jesus could have asked us to do

115
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:33,640
on a regular basis, why communion?

116
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:35,880
It seems strange, it seems odd.

117
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:38,180
And then how should we approach the table?

118
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:38,860
What is communion?

119
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:41,080
Why did Jesus give it to us?

120
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:44,840
And how should we participate in it?

121
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:45,900
And again, I personally,

122
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:47,260
I thought when I got this question

123
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:49,520
that it was gonna be a softball for me, right?

124
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:51,420
I've grown up in the church.

125
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:52,640
I know what communion is.

126
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:53,760
I know about communion.

127
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:56,540
I take communion, I've led communion services.

128
00:04:56,540 --> 00:04:58,940
It should be an easy message for me to prepare.

129
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:01,440
But the more that I studied,

130
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:04,200
the more I realized that communion is much bigger

131
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,720
and more beautiful than I've often given it credit for.

132
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:09,160
And so my hope, my prayer is that that will happen

133
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:11,400
for many of us in the room this morning.

134
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:14,960
What exactly is communion?

135
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:17,540
Well, communion is one of the two primary ordinances

136
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:20,960
or sacraments given by God to the church.

137
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:24,380
Some expressions of the church throughout history

138
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:26,560
have emphasized more than two,

139
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:29,620
but the majority of the church throughout all of history

140
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:32,240
has agreed on two primary ordinances.

141
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:36,560
An ordinance is just a specific practice

142
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:39,540
given to the church by God to do together.

143
00:05:39,540 --> 00:05:41,320
The first is baptism.

144
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,820
It's a one-time ordinance for new believers

145
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:45,340
to do in front of their church community.

146
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:47,660
The thing that I love about both ordinances

147
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:50,820
is that it is both an individual and a corporate act

148
00:05:50,820 --> 00:05:54,280
because Christianity is not an individual faith.

149
00:05:54,280 --> 00:05:55,940
It is a corporate faith.

150
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:57,360
We do this thing together.

151
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:59,080
So when we do baptisms,

152
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:00,880
which we're going to do later in November,

153
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:04,200
individuals are going to participate

154
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:05,660
in the act of baptism,

155
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:07,940
the ordinance, the sacrament of baptism,

156
00:06:07,940 --> 00:06:10,020
and we are going to be observers

157
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:11,680
and celebrators of that

158
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:14,920
because we are in this together with them.

159
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:16,900
The second is communion.

160
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:19,300
It is a repetitive practice for the church

161
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:23,160
to do regularly with one another in community.

162
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:28,520
I love what this old dead guy says about sacraments.

163
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:29,800
His name's Thomas Watson.

164
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:34,280
He says a sacrament is a visible sermon.

165
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:37,080
It is a physical action

166
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:39,240
that invites individuals and communities

167
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:42,840
to participate in spiritual truths.

168
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:45,600
I've often said I love when we do communion

169
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:47,400
at the end of services that I preach

170
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:50,020
because even if I totally botch the message,

171
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:53,600
you will be able to see the beauty and grace of Jesus

172
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:55,940
in the bread and the cup.

173
00:06:57,180 --> 00:07:00,320
Communion is a great way to save a bad sermon.

174
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:07,320
So in order to succinctly answer this question,

175
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,200
what exactly is communion?

176
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:10,160
I think what I want to do

177
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:11,700
is I want to go over the five titles

178
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:14,700
that the Bible uses for communion

179
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:16,320
and draw out some applications.

180
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:18,840
I'm going to try and go through these quickly, hopefully,

181
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:22,580
because I want to get some more time

182
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:23,340
in the why and the how.

183
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:26,760
So five titles we find in Scripture for communion.

184
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:29,460
The first is the Lord's Supper.

185
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:32,840
We see this in 1 Corinthians 11, verse 20.

186
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:35,100
When you meet together, Paul says,

187
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:37,840
you are not really interested in the Lord's Supper.

188
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:41,280
We're going to get to the Corinthians in a little bit.

189
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:43,040
Paul doesn't seem very happy with him,

190
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:47,100
but this is a title that the early church uses for communion.

191
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:50,280
What's beautiful about the Lord's Supper

192
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:53,280
is that what Jesus does in the Lord's Supper

193
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:55,680
is he institutes a new meal

194
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:58,000
that replaces a very familiar meal

195
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,680
to the Jewish community.

196
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:05,960
See, when Jesus first ordained the act of communion,

197
00:08:05,960 --> 00:08:07,340
he did so on the night

198
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:10,300
where him and his followers were celebrating Passover.

199
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:13,540
Jesus in the Lord's Supper

200
00:08:13,540 --> 00:08:17,060
is moving forward God's redemptive plan

201
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:20,160
from Israel to the church.

202
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,860
Jesus meticulously crafted this final meal with his disciples.

203
00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:25,740
If you've ever read the story

204
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:28,920
about the night that the Lord's Supper happened

205
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:29,780
for the first time,

206
00:08:29,780 --> 00:08:31,680
it's kind of like a spy movie.

207
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:34,060
Jesus tells his disciples,

208
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:35,720
like, hey, go into this town

209
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:37,580
and look for a guy with a pot

210
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:38,660
and follow him

211
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:41,080
and all of the stuff's gonna be set up, right?

212
00:08:41,080 --> 00:08:42,440
It's, like, really cryptic.

213
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:44,740
But, like, it shows that Jesus

214
00:08:44,740 --> 00:08:46,740
put so much thought and preparation

215
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:49,620
into setting up this Passover meal

216
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:51,640
so that he could redefine for his disciples.

217
00:08:51,640 --> 00:08:52,800
Right before he went to the cross,

218
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:54,020
he could redefine for them

219
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:56,700
what this meal was all about.

220
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:59,040
The Lord's Supper was moving forward

221
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:00,200
God's redemptive plan

222
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:01,860
from the old covenant to the new covenant.

223
00:09:01,860 --> 00:09:05,060
Passover was all about looking back to Yahweh,

224
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:07,220
freeing Israel from slavery in Egypt

225
00:09:07,220 --> 00:09:09,320
and awaiting their ultimate savior

226
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:11,320
promised by their prophets.

227
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:13,420
Jesus takes this meal

228
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:15,520
and he transforms it into a new meal,

229
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:17,000
the Lord's Supper.

230
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,120
And this emphasizes the ultimate fulfillment

231
00:09:19,120 --> 00:09:20,280
of God's saving work.

232
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:22,480
When Jesus instituted the Lord's Supper,

233
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:23,200
he was saying,

234
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:25,800
your awaited salvation has come through me.

235
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:28,320
I am the ultimate Passover lamb

236
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:30,140
who won't just bring earthly salvation

237
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:31,860
to Israel from physical enemies,

238
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:33,900
but I will bring eternal salvation

239
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:36,320
to the whole world from spiritual enemies.

240
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:38,940
Jesus moving forward,

241
00:09:38,940 --> 00:09:40,520
the redemptive plan of God.

242
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:47,980
It reimagines what salvation really is all about.

243
00:09:47,980 --> 00:09:51,000
And it looks forward to his future salvation.

244
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,300
In Passover, Passover looked backwards

245
00:09:53,300 --> 00:09:57,180
to God setting Israel free from Egypt

246
00:09:57,180 --> 00:09:59,860
and it looked forward to a coming Messiah.

247
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:02,020
Communion, the Lord's Supper,

248
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:04,980
it has both a looking back and a looking forward.

249
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:08,220
Now we look back to Jesus who is our Passover lamb

250
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:10,540
and we look forward to the marriage supper of the lamb

251
00:10:10,540 --> 00:10:11,920
when Jesus will return

252
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:14,720
and he will have this meal with us once again.

253
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:17,120
Listen to it in Matthew chapter 26, verse 29.

254
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:17,760
Jesus says,

255
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:28,180
So the Lord's Supper communion,

256
00:10:28,180 --> 00:10:30,160
it's about looking backwards to the cross,

257
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:33,140
what Jesus did through his death, burial, and resurrection.

258
00:10:33,140 --> 00:10:37,800
It's about thinking that he is the new Passover lamb,

259
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:42,500
the Passover lamb that became the sacrifice for our sins once and for all.

260
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:45,480
And it's also about looking forward to a future salvation.

261
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:47,840
We're getting ready to turn our attention to Advent.

262
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:52,920
And Advent is all about if Jesus came the first time like the prophets promised,

263
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:56,000
then he will come the second time like he promised us.

264
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,660
The Lord's Supper is both a looking back and a looking forward.

265
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:03,800
It demonstrates God's plan of salvation through Jesus,

266
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,180
the ultimate Passover lamb.

267
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:07,000
It unifies the old and new covenants

268
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,120
and prophetically looks forward to his final salvation

269
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:13,000
when he will bring heaven to earth once and for all.

270
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,960
Second title of communion in the Scriptures is the breaking of bread.

271
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:21,360
We see this in Acts 2, 42.

272
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:21,900
It says,

273
00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:25,440
They devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and to fellowship

274
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:28,360
and to the breaking of bread and to prayer.

275
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:33,260
Almost all theologians believe that this title,

276
00:11:33,260 --> 00:11:35,520
breaking of bread, was a title for communion.

277
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:39,980
It doesn't really make sense to say fellowship and breaking of bread

278
00:11:39,980 --> 00:11:42,880
unless the breaking of bread signified something specific

279
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:44,320
and tangible that they were doing.

280
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:46,980
This title, breaking of bread,

281
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:50,960
it emphasizes Christ's sacrificial death to secure our salvation

282
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:55,260
and join us together in a new multi-ethnic family of God

283
00:11:55,260 --> 00:11:57,620
from every tribe, language, and tongue.

284
00:11:57,620 --> 00:12:01,300
Jesus, when he instituted this meal,

285
00:12:01,300 --> 00:12:03,940
he took the bread and he broke it.

286
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:05,420
He said,

287
00:12:05,420 --> 00:12:09,500
This is my body, which is broken for you.

288
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:15,880
This title reminds us of the cost that this meal took

289
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:17,560
in order to be instituted,

290
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:20,260
that Christ's body had to be broken for us

291
00:12:20,260 --> 00:12:23,620
in order for us to be invited to this table.

292
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:25,860
God made Jesus, who knew no sin,

293
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:27,680
to be an offering for our sins

294
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:29,620
so that we can sit at the table.

295
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:33,920
We can come to the table and receive grace and mercy and forgiveness

296
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:37,140
and reconciled relationships with one another.

297
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:42,480
Breaking of bread reminds us that Christ is hosting a meal

298
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:44,320
and he's inviting you to the table

299
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:47,700
and the cost of that meal was his very life.

300
00:12:47,700 --> 00:12:52,740
Think about, I mean, just think about how much it means to you,

301
00:12:52,740 --> 00:12:57,280
unless you're like the most introverted human being of all time.

302
00:12:57,280 --> 00:12:59,180
Think about how much it means to you

303
00:12:59,180 --> 00:13:01,880
when someone invites you over to their home for a meal.

304
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:04,280
Think about how significant it is.

305
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:06,640
You get there and they've totally prepared everything.

306
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:07,720
They've thought about everything.

307
00:13:07,920 --> 00:13:09,360
They've answered all of your questions.

308
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:11,080
They've got appetizers, right?

309
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:14,160
Like it just makes you feel loved, accepted, wanted.

310
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:17,900
And this is what Jesus is doing at communion,

311
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:22,060
except the cost of the meal with his very own body and blood.

312
00:13:22,060 --> 00:13:25,180
Jesus is inviting you to his table

313
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:28,240
and he was willing to pay the ultimate sacrifice.

314
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:30,180
And this is one of those ones,

315
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:34,440
I love emphasizing the communal aspect of communion,

316
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:35,400
but this is one of those ones

317
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,080
I really want you to think about personally.

318
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:43,760
Jesus, his body was broken and his blood was shed

319
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:47,440
so that you could be invited to this table.

320
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:50,740
Like personalize that for a minute.

321
00:13:50,740 --> 00:13:53,160
I don't know about you, but I tend to think,

322
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:54,300
yeah, Jesus loved the whole world.

323
00:13:54,300 --> 00:13:55,900
No, Jesus loved you.

324
00:13:56,480 --> 00:13:58,680
And he invites you to this table

325
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:01,860
at the cost of his very body and blood.

326
00:14:01,860 --> 00:14:04,880
The breaking of bread, the Lord's Supper.

327
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:07,360
I skipped three slides.

328
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:08,200
That's not good.

329
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:10,460
Number three, the Eucharist.

330
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:14,040
Anybody grow up with this title, Eucharist?

331
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:17,180
Okay, it's a bit fancier.

332
00:14:17,180 --> 00:14:19,400
Nothing to be afraid of.

333
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:22,380
Just comes from the Greek word for thanksgiving.

334
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:26,080
In 1 Corinthians 11, verse 23,

335
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:27,620
for I pass on to you what I received.

336
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:30,300
The Lord himself on the night when he was betrayed,

337
00:14:30,300 --> 00:14:34,320
he took some bread and he gave thanks to God for it.

338
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:35,460
Then he broke it.

339
00:14:35,460 --> 00:14:38,640
Eucharist just comes from the Greek word for thanksgiving.

340
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:41,700
It reminds us that the communion table,

341
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:44,020
the Lord's Supper was instituted

342
00:14:44,020 --> 00:14:48,160
at the end of a very long meal around a table.

343
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:51,420
There's things to give thanks for.

344
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:53,660
And although there are somber undertones

345
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:56,040
on the night of the first communion,

346
00:14:56,040 --> 00:14:57,580
Jesus gave thanks.

347
00:14:57,580 --> 00:15:00,060
This is something that we can do

348
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:02,460
with celebration in our hearts.

349
00:15:02,460 --> 00:15:05,080
We give thanks to God for who Jesus is

350
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:06,700
and for what he did.

351
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:08,580
Communion is intended to be reflective,

352
00:15:08,580 --> 00:15:11,840
but it's also intended to be filled with thanksgiving.

353
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:15,500
Jesus gave thanks for the opportunity

354
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:18,680
to participate in this meal with one another.

355
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:21,920
We should be thankful when we come to the table.

356
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:23,020
We should look around the room

357
00:15:23,020 --> 00:15:25,200
and give thanksgiving for the people

358
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:27,120
that God has brought around this table.

359
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:30,040
In many churches I have been a part of

360
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:32,260
and even communions that I have led

361
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:34,420
sometimes tend to overemphasize

362
00:15:34,420 --> 00:15:35,740
the weightiness of communion.

363
00:15:35,740 --> 00:15:38,360
And I think that's important.

364
00:15:38,360 --> 00:15:39,380
That's an important element,

365
00:15:39,380 --> 00:15:40,520
but it's both and.

366
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:43,560
It's something that we should have joyful attitudes

367
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:44,400
in our heart towards

368
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:46,040
that we should have thanksgiving for.

369
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:48,140
So we've got the Lord's Supper,

370
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:49,680
the breaking of bread, Eucharist,

371
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:52,840
the weirdest one of all, the love feast.

372
00:15:52,840 --> 00:15:54,780
Everybody loves the love feast.

373
00:15:54,780 --> 00:15:58,100
We see this in Jude 12.

374
00:15:58,100 --> 00:16:00,280
If you've never read Jude before, don't worry.

375
00:16:00,280 --> 00:16:01,420
I'm sure you're not alone.

376
00:16:01,420 --> 00:16:03,480
I heard one pastor call it

377
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:05,320
one of the Bible's deep tracks,

378
00:16:05,320 --> 00:16:07,540
which I thought was awesome.

379
00:16:07,540 --> 00:16:10,400
Do yourself a favor, read Jude sometime,

380
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:12,560
but they use this term, the love feast,

381
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:13,580
to refer to communion.

382
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:15,960
And this term for communion is one

383
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:17,440
that has been used historically

384
00:16:17,440 --> 00:16:19,100
by the expression of churches

385
00:16:19,100 --> 00:16:20,260
that we are a part of.

386
00:16:20,260 --> 00:16:22,920
The Brethren Church, the Anabaptist Church

387
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:25,020
have often used this term, the love feast.

388
00:16:25,020 --> 00:16:26,160
The reason they do that

389
00:16:26,160 --> 00:16:29,820
is because they have intentionally chosen

390
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:32,520
to include both a meal and,

391
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:33,380
drum roll please.

392
00:16:33,380 --> 00:16:36,460
Thank you, thank you.

393
00:16:36,460 --> 00:16:39,360
Foot washing with the bread and the cup.

394
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:42,980
Much of, so they, this love feast

395
00:16:42,980 --> 00:16:45,020
is kind of this all-encompassing,

396
00:16:45,020 --> 00:16:49,240
a full meal, the bread broken,

397
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:51,820
the cup shared, and then a foot washing.

398
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:55,260
And much of the church throughout history

399
00:16:55,260 --> 00:16:58,200
has viewed Christ's invitation to wash feet

400
00:16:58,200 --> 00:17:00,480
as kind of this cultural expression

401
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:01,500
of sacrificial love,

402
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:02,700
that Jesus is inviting us

403
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:04,740
to be sacrificially loving towards one another,

404
00:17:05,100 --> 00:17:06,140
and the foot washing thing

405
00:17:06,140 --> 00:17:09,180
was just a cultural example that he used.

406
00:17:09,180 --> 00:17:13,400
But, what if it was actually an invitation

407
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:15,460
for all believers throughout history

408
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:18,240
to practice the most challenging aspect

409
00:17:18,240 --> 00:17:19,560
of the Christian life,

410
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:22,140
which is to humbly serve

411
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:24,880
and to be served?

412
00:17:24,880 --> 00:17:28,000
Listen to Jesus in John chapter 13.

413
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,240
On this night that he instituted the Lord's Supper,

414
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:32,260
after he washed his disciples' feet,

415
00:17:32,260 --> 00:17:33,100
he says this,

416
00:17:33,100 --> 00:17:35,640
if I then, your Lord and teacher,

417
00:17:35,640 --> 00:17:36,800
have washed your feet,

418
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:40,120
you also ought to wash one another's feet.

419
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:42,580
For I have given you an example

420
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:46,100
that you also should do just as I have done to you.

421
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:48,580
Truly, truly, I say to you,

422
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:50,800
a servant is not greater than his master,

423
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:53,700
nor is a messenger greater than the one who sent him.

424
00:17:53,700 --> 00:17:55,980
If you know these things,

425
00:17:55,980 --> 00:17:59,760
blessed are you who do something like them.

426
00:18:00,440 --> 00:18:06,180
Okay, blessed are you if you do them.

427
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:08,960
Now, I don't have time to give this view justice,

428
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:11,480
and we have actually intentionally planned

429
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:13,180
an entire special service

430
00:18:13,180 --> 00:18:15,120
around giving this view justice.

431
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:17,240
There should be an insert in your flyer.

432
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:19,380
I would love for you,

433
00:18:19,380 --> 00:18:20,840
if this interests you at all,

434
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:22,380
even if it doesn't interest you at all,

435
00:18:22,380 --> 00:18:23,720
if you think this is crazy,

436
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:25,800
I would challenge you,

437
00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:27,140
I would even dare you

438
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:30,520
to come to our fall feast.

439
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:33,120
We are bringing in a brethren theologian,

440
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:34,520
a seminary professor

441
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:37,080
who is far smarter than I could ever be,

442
00:18:37,080 --> 00:18:38,840
and he is going to talk

443
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:41,120
about the significance of the love feast,

444
00:18:41,120 --> 00:18:43,800
of why our churches historically

445
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:45,380
have included a full meal

446
00:18:45,380 --> 00:18:46,300
and a foot washing

447
00:18:46,300 --> 00:18:49,920
in at least one or two communions a year.

448
00:18:50,580 --> 00:18:51,900
We're gonna share a meal together.

449
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:52,960
We're gonna listen to a teaching

450
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:54,340
from a theological scholar.

451
00:18:54,340 --> 00:18:56,640
I know some of you love the heady stuff.

452
00:18:56,640 --> 00:18:59,360
Dare you, I dare you.

453
00:18:59,360 --> 00:19:01,360
I don't know if you can tell,

454
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:03,020
but I'm nervous about people coming to this thing,

455
00:19:03,020 --> 00:19:04,000
so I'm daring everybody.

456
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:05,280
I dare you.

457
00:19:05,280 --> 00:19:07,560
And what we're gonna do

458
00:19:07,560 --> 00:19:08,460
is we're gonna share a meal together.

459
00:19:08,460 --> 00:19:09,440
We're gonna listen to a teaching,

460
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:11,460
and we're going to have an opportunity

461
00:19:11,460 --> 00:19:12,820
to wash feet.

462
00:19:12,820 --> 00:19:14,240
It will be optional.

463
00:19:14,240 --> 00:19:15,440
So you can come for the meal

464
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:16,100
and the teaching,

465
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:17,080
and then you can just

466
00:19:17,080 --> 00:19:19,080
watch other people wash feet,

467
00:19:19,080 --> 00:19:21,620
which might be weirder than washing feet.

468
00:19:21,620 --> 00:19:23,740
I'm not sure.

469
00:19:23,740 --> 00:19:29,500
And this is a very new cultural experience

470
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:30,300
for a lot of us.

471
00:19:30,300 --> 00:19:31,560
All I can tell you

472
00:19:31,560 --> 00:19:32,680
is from personal experience,

473
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:33,860
I have participated

474
00:19:33,860 --> 00:19:35,680
in a few foot washing services,

475
00:19:35,680 --> 00:19:37,200
and they are some of the most powerful,

476
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:40,500
moving, emotional, challenging services

477
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:42,120
I've ever been a part of.

478
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:44,560
So I don't have time to do it justice.

479
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:45,880
Someone else is gonna do it justice

480
00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:46,520
in a few weeks.

481
00:19:46,520 --> 00:19:47,780
Please make plans to attend.

482
00:19:47,780 --> 00:19:50,720
Finally, communion.

483
00:19:50,720 --> 00:19:54,440
Communion is probably my favorite term

484
00:19:54,440 --> 00:19:56,740
for the Lord's Supper.

485
00:19:56,740 --> 00:19:59,420
It comes from 1 Corinthians 10,

486
00:19:59,420 --> 00:20:00,700
verses 16 and 17.

487
00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:01,300
Paul says,

488
00:20:01,300 --> 00:20:03,520
when we bless the cup at the Lord's table,

489
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:07,240
are we not sharing in the blood of Christ?

490
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:10,660
Let's not read past that.

491
00:20:10,660 --> 00:20:12,300
That's a significant,

492
00:20:12,300 --> 00:20:15,260
that is a significant claim of Paul.

493
00:20:15,260 --> 00:20:18,900
Are we not sharing in the blood of Christ?

494
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:20,260
And when we break the bed,

495
00:20:20,260 --> 00:20:23,860
are we not sharing in the body of Christ?

496
00:20:23,860 --> 00:20:25,460
The Greek word there for sharing,

497
00:20:25,460 --> 00:20:27,860
many other translations use communion.

498
00:20:27,860 --> 00:20:29,320
It is koinonia.

499
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:31,860
This is deeply,

500
00:20:31,860 --> 00:20:35,360
this is a deep and intimate participatory word.

501
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:38,040
We are invited to commune with one another

502
00:20:38,040 --> 00:20:40,380
and with the body and blood of Christ

503
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:44,100
in some supernatural and mysterious way.

504
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:47,440
This is significant.

505
00:20:47,440 --> 00:20:48,620
In the Western church,

506
00:20:48,620 --> 00:20:50,680
we struggle with understanding

507
00:20:50,680 --> 00:20:54,200
how spiritual things and physical things overlap.

508
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:55,820
Am I the only one?

509
00:20:55,820 --> 00:20:58,920
We often consider them separate spheres

510
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:00,540
that don't overlap with one another.

511
00:21:00,540 --> 00:21:03,880
This is platonic and not biblical, okay?

512
00:21:03,880 --> 00:21:06,360
So when we take communion,

513
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:09,060
we are invited into an intimate time of fellowship

514
00:21:09,060 --> 00:21:11,240
with God and with one another.

515
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:14,680
We are reminded that even when we struggle

516
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:16,180
to get along or be united,

517
00:21:16,180 --> 00:21:19,400
look at verse 17.

518
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:21,500
And though we are many,

519
00:21:21,500 --> 00:21:24,220
we all eat from one loaf of bread

520
00:21:24,220 --> 00:21:26,780
showing that we are one body.

521
00:21:26,780 --> 00:21:31,420
So no matter how hard relationships get in this room,

522
00:21:31,420 --> 00:21:34,080
especially around election time,

523
00:21:34,080 --> 00:21:36,720
no matter how many external factors

524
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:38,660
cause us to want to divide from one another,

525
00:21:38,660 --> 00:21:41,060
we are invited to come to the same table

526
00:21:41,060 --> 00:21:43,200
and take from the same loaf of bread,

527
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:47,120
which is a representation that we are one body,

528
00:21:47,120 --> 00:21:48,920
unified around Christ,

529
00:21:49,080 --> 00:21:51,820
body broken for us and his blood shed for us.

530
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:53,780
If we can't unify on anything else,

531
00:21:53,780 --> 00:21:55,380
God help us if we can't unify

532
00:21:55,380 --> 00:21:58,540
on the death, burial, and resurrection of Jesus.

533
00:21:58,540 --> 00:22:05,940
Let's say that communion is a physical,

534
00:22:05,940 --> 00:22:08,560
spiritual, and prophetic remembering

535
00:22:08,560 --> 00:22:11,260
of Christ's body with Christ's body.

536
00:22:11,260 --> 00:22:13,740
Something we do physically

537
00:22:13,740 --> 00:22:16,640
that has spiritual implications and future prophetic.

538
00:22:16,640 --> 00:22:18,580
We're looking forward to the return of Christ.

539
00:22:19,080 --> 00:22:21,640
When we participate in remembering Christ's body

540
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:23,180
with Christ's body.

541
00:22:23,180 --> 00:22:27,000
Which leads us to the why of communion.

542
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:31,740
And this was the thrust of the question I received.

543
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:32,860
